WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.651 --> 00:00:02.114
- I love hearing my name.

00:00:02.114 --> 00:00:03.364
I love my name.

00:00:04.368 --> 00:00:05.451
I don't care if that's vain,

00:00:05.451 --> 00:00:07.720
'cause I spent a lot
of time hating my name.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:10.960
- [Narrator] Our names
are powerful, literally.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.460
Our brains have a unique reaction
to the sound of our names,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.560
sometimes even while sedated,

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.100
but many students have their names

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.190
mispronounced or disregarded
every day in school.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.080
So what impact does that
have on their education?

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.000
And what can educators do?

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:26.964
- [Woman] My name is Adenis.

00:00:26.964 --> 00:00:27.894
- Ajani.
- Jamara.

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:28.727
- Javia.

00:00:28.727 --> 00:00:29.902
- Nekbakht.
- Alilia.

00:00:29.902 --> 00:00:32.006
- Nadesh Visimungu. 

00:00:32.006 --> 00:00:33.260
- Raghdan.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.883
- It's actually Nichbacht, not
Nichbat, Nichbich, Nichbod.

00:00:39.254 --> 00:00:40.703
(bright music)

00:00:40.703 --> 00:00:42.530
- [Lilia] Two of the embarrassing

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.070
the moment when there's a pause

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.860
and you know it's 'cause of you.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.360
- [Raghdan] It just goes so far

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.589
that sometimes it's even concerning.

00:00:49.589 --> 00:00:51.570
Like, Rajakada.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.978
- [Amara] I remember just
being like, "Oh, that must be."

00:00:53.978 --> 00:00:56.150
- Especially if they said David.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:57.750
Everyone would laugh.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.720
- [Ajani] I learned pretty quickly

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.680
that it was easier to just go by AJ,

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.110
which is something that I, quite honestly,

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:04.573
regret doing now.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.210
- [Narrator] In 2012,
researchers Doctor Rita Kohli

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.067
and Doctor Daniel Solorrzzano
published a study called,

00:01:13.067 --> 00:01:15.040
"Teachers, please learn our names!:

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.060
racial mioxcroaggressions
and the K-12 classroom."

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.580
Here's Doctor Kohli.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.440
- [Doctor Kohli] Schools are
not a power-neutral space.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.600
Teachers are an authority figure.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.650
Teachers how power in those contexts.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.360
And so, often, what they say goes.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.620
Often, what they say is viewed as

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.620
the legitimate knowledge in the space.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:33.680
- [Narrator] So it takes a lot

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.877
for a first grader to
say to their teacher,

00:01:35.877 --> 00:01:38.277
"Excuse me, you pronounced
my name this way,

00:01:38.277 --> 00:01:40.007
"but it's actually this way

00:01:40.007 --> 00:01:41.540
"and it matters to me."

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.170
In fact, it takes a lot
for a student of any age.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:48.200
- The first time I ever
tried to confront a professor

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.640
was actually last semester,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.490
and it wasn't really a good experience.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.050
- Some professors

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.190
just don't even ask.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.530
They're just like, "Can
I call you by this name?"

00:01:58.530 --> 00:01:59.870
- She mispronounced my name

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.560
then asked me if she can
call me something else.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.320
- Regardless of whether we want

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.280
to be called a certain way or not,

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.450
it's an elder person

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.440
who has a PhD, and this, and that.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.910
- Later on, I felt like
it was my responsibility

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.693
to actually email and tell her that,

00:02:15.547 --> 00:02:17.617
"Professor, I get that it's
the first day of class,

00:02:17.617 --> 00:02:19.967
"but you don't even have the right

00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:22.150
"to ask me if you can
call me "something else."

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:24.170
She later on apologized,

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:27.580
but it's only because we pushed
the conversation forward.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.663
- [Narrator] So, what is the big deal?

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.010
- If you can't understand my name

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.603
how can you understand me?

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:40.770
- [Narrator] A microaggression
is a subtle form

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.290
of discrimination that can occur

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.470
intentionally or unintentionally,

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.140
and it can include telling a student

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.170
their name is too hard to say.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.540
- We're centering a particular
cultural vantage point

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.560
when we say things are
difficult to pronounce,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:53.430
because they're not really

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.820
difficult to pronounce inherently.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.450
They're difficult to pronounce for us.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.450
- [Narrator] Doctor Kohli's
research underscores

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.780
that a person's name is their
entrance point to the world.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.070
So, what happens when those
names are mispronounced,

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.490
disregarded, or even mocked?

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.323
- [Doctor Kohli] They start to feel

00:03:08.323 --> 00:03:09.520
like their name was a burden,

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.220
they start to feel like they
themselves were a burden,

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.900
and so they begin to withdraw sometimes

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:18.350
from participating socially
and academically in schools.

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:19.400
- [Narrator] There's actually a term

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.310
for the unintended lessons
kids learn in school.

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:25.030
Things that live between
the lines, and in hallways,

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:26.903
and in roll call.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:30.720
- [Al-Jawhara] There's
a lot that we impart

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.270
onto our students or onto each other

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.360
that is complete subtext.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.230
We don't say it out loud.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.650
- [Narrator] Al-Jawhara Al-Thani
is the head of educational

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.540
and community programs
at Qatar Foundation.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.560
Her work focuses on hidden curriculum

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:44.960
and how to fix it.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.063
- [Al-Jawhara] I was in a class once,

00:03:47.063 --> 00:03:51.230
a sixth grade all boys English class,

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.330
and it was like like 15
minutes quiet reading time.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:54.610
I was sitting at the back of the class.

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:57.250
At one point, the boy puts his hand up

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:58.687
and asks the teacher, he's like,

00:03:58.687 --> 00:04:00.070
"Sir, I have a question."

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:00.903
He goes, "Yeah?"

00:04:00.903 --> 00:04:03.200
He goes, "Why are they all white?"

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.800
You think kids don't register?

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.320
It registers.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:07.850
- [Narrator] There are
decades worth of studies

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.960
examining how curriculum,
sometimes the world over,

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:14.400
is primarily Euro-centric,
male, and upper class

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.130
with names to match.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.480
It registers.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.390
But, the curriculum is
not always something

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:21.410
that's up to a teacher themselves.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.943
Learning names, on the other hand, is.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.810
- You're sitting in the
back row in a lecture

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.450
of 200 students meeting on your phone.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:33.117
You're gonna get me calling you by name,

00:04:33.117 --> 00:04:34.690
"What are you doing?"

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.610
- [Narrator] Professor
Susan Dun has been teaching

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.990
at Northwestern University
in Qatar for over a decade.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:42.370
Her undergrad experience
at a liberal arts college

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:44.600
with small classes influenced her decision

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.470
to memorize the names of
every student in her classes,

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:48.690
wherever she teaches.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.560
Even in the 200-person lectures.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.460
- [Susan] For me, teaching
is about a lot more

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:54.490
than just transmission of knowledge.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.180
It's about the growth and development

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:57.400
of human beings,

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.880
and so I can be far more
effective in doing that

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.820
if I have at least a recognition of

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.740
know the identity of
people, know their names,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.050
know a little bit of something about them.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:11.780
Even in a large class.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.950
That they know when they
walk in and I say, "Hi,"

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.960
and use their name, that I
really know who they are.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.700
- When I hear someone
say my name correctly,

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:21.757
I'd be like, "Oh my god.

00:05:21.757 --> 00:05:24.167
"Finally this person!

00:05:24.167 --> 00:05:26.370
"They know how to say my name!"

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:29.610
- I think in our
increasingly globalized world

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.500
where we have people from all sorts of

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.710
walks of life interacting,

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:35.640
that the idea that
we're gonna mispronounce

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.348
each other's names is pretty much a given.

00:05:38.348 --> 00:05:41.180
And so the question and the challenge is

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:43.710
to do as much as we can to figure out

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.010
how to embrace each other,

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:48.000
which sometimes starts with simply

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.500
taking the time and effort to figure out

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.010
how to greet each other properly.

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:55.160
- Why we're not asking teachers

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.080
to be able to say
everybody's name correctly

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.300
the second they walk through the door,

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.300
but I think there's a difference between

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:05.660
imposing that, "Oh, your
name is hard to say,"

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:09.857
versus, "I haven't learned how
to say a name like this yet,

00:06:09.857 --> 00:06:12.177
"but I'm working on it
because you're important,

00:06:12.177 --> 00:06:15.907
"and your name is important,
and I want to honor that

00:06:15.907 --> 00:06:17.430
"in my classroom."

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:19.560
- [Narrator] What working
on it looks like in practice

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.700
might be different for every educator.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:23.790
What's key is allowing teachers the room

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:25.560
to be students in this too.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:26.680
- If we flip this around

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.080
and we think about the
expectation for students

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.600
to learn lots of new things
all at the same time,

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:34.330
and we encourage them to fail,

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:35.730
and we encourage them to keep trying,

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:36.870
and we encourage them,

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.440
I think that vulnerability
that we expect of students

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:42.473
should be the same expectation
that we have of our teachers.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:45.050
They aren't the holders of all knowledge.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:45.883
We know that.

00:06:45.883 --> 00:06:47.010
We know that they're human.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.830
- [Davia] What would I
tell my younger self?

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:55.810
I would tell her to be proud
that you have a unique name.

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:59.770
- [Raghdan] The fact that
you have such a unique name

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:03.000
doesn't make you stand
out in a negative way,

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.710
but instead it makes you shine.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:07.320
- Know that's like snithing, embarrassing.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:09.790
- It's gonna take time,
but you will understand

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:12.390
how important your name is actually,

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:15.700
and how your parents did not name you

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.830
just for the sake of naming someone.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:19.510
- I would tell my younger self

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:22.080
to learn about the
significance of your name

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:25.217
and call that teacher out when
they mispronounce your name.

00:07:25.217 --> 00:07:26.900
- I would probably just

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:29.000
take my younger self by the shoulders,

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.527
look him dead in the eye, and
tell him, "Your name means

00:07:31.527 --> 00:07:34.050
"He who wins the struggle, and it'll be

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:35.657
a prophesy if you let it be."

